<section class="summit-section summit-section--grid" id="installs">
  <div class="summit-container">
    <span class="summit-eyebrow">01 / Heat pump installation</span>
    <h2>Cold-climate heat pumps, <em>sized honestly.</em></h2>
    <p class="summit-lead">Colorado winters demand cold-climate performance — units that maintain rated capacity down to 5&deg;F or below. We install Mitsubishi Hyper-Heat, Bosch IDS, Carrier Infinity Greenspeed, Daikin Fit, and Trane XV series, matched to your Manual J. Every install includes commissioning documentation.</p>

    <div class="summit-services">
      <article class="summit-service">
        <div class="summit-service-label">Equipment &middot; ducted</div>
        <h3>Ducted heat pump + backup</h3>
        <p>Whole-home ducted system with electric-resistance or gas furnace backup for cold-snap redundancy. Pairs with existing duct work when feasible; redesigns when it isn't.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="summit-service">
        <div class="summit-service-label">Equipment &middot; ductless</div>
        <h3>Mini-split (multi-zone)</h3>
        <p>Room-by-room comfort without duct losses. Ideal for additions, finished basements, historic homes, or any space where running duct is worse than the system cost.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="summit-service">
        <div class="summit-service-label">Equipment &middot; hybrid</div>
        <h3>Dual-fuel heat pump + furnace</h3>
        <p>Heat pump handles 85% of the heating hours; existing or new furnace takes over below a set outdoor temp. Best-of-both for homes with a recently replaced furnace we don't need to tear out.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="summit-section summit-section--pale" id="ac">
  <div class="summit-container">
    <span class="summit-eyebrow">02 / AC replacement</span>
    <h2>Replacement that <em>lowers</em> the bill.</h2>
    <p class="summit-lead">Most replacement AC installs are drop-in swaps of the exact same tonnage the previous contractor sold the homeowner. That's how 4-ton systems end up in 1,800 sf ranches. We recalculate the load, right-size the unit, and usually find the correct tonnage is a full ton smaller.</p>
    <div class="summit-services">
      <article class="summit-service">
        <div class="summit-service-label">Equipment &middot; standard</div>
        <h3>Single-stage AC</h3>
        <p>For tight budgets and smaller homes where the efficiency bump of a variable-speed unit doesn't pay back. 14.3 SEER2 minimum per code.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="summit-service">
        <div class="summit-service-label">Equipment &middot; two-stage</div>
        <h3>Two-stage AC</h3>
        <p>Runs at 70% capacity most of the time, full capacity on the hottest days. Quieter, better dehumidification, 15&ndash;17 SEER2 range. The residential default for 2,000&ndash;3,500 sf homes.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="summit-service">
        <div class="summit-service-label">Equipment &middot; variable</div>
        <h3>Variable-speed AC</h3>
        <p>Inverter-driven compressor modulates continuously. 18&ndash;26 SEER2. Premium cost, premium quiet, premium humidity control. Pays back fastest in high-usage homes.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="summit-section" id="furnace">
  <div class="summit-container">
    <span class="summit-eyebrow">03 / Furnace repair + replacement</span>
    <h2>Honest diagnostics, <em>flat-rate repairs.</em></h2>
    <p class="summit-lead">We don't sell you a new furnace when yours has an $80 flame sensor failure. Diagnostic fee is $89 and waived on any repair over $400. Same-day service available Mon&ndash;Sat during heating season.</p>
    <div class="summit-services">
      <article class="summit-service">
        <div class="summit-service-label">Common repairs</div>
        <h3>Igniters, sensors, valves</h3>
        <p>Flame sensor cleaning $110. Hot-surface igniter replacement $180. Pressure switch $195. Gas valve $420&ndash;$680. Most repairs done on the first visit.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="summit-service">
        <div class="summit-service-label">System repairs</div>
        <h3>Inducer motors, boards</h3>
        <p>Inducer assembly $620. Control board $380&ndash;$520. Variable-speed blower motor $720. Parts stocked on the truck for the top 20 residential brands.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="summit-service">
        <div class="summit-service-label">When to replace</div>
        <h3>Replacement threshold</h3>
        <p>We'll tell you honestly if a repair is throwing good money after bad. Rule of thumb: if repair cost &times; age in years exceeds $5,000, we recommend replacement. You decide.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="summit-section summit-section--pale" id="air">
  <div class="summit-container">
    <span class="summit-eyebrow">04 / Indoor air quality</span>
    <h2>Filtration, <em>humidification, ventilation.</em></h2>
    <p class="summit-lead">Front Range winters are dry; summers are increasingly smoky. Indoor air quality is a three-part problem — particulates, humidity, and fresh-air ventilation. We size and install each independently.</p>
    <div class="summit-services">
      <article class="summit-service">
        <div class="summit-service-label">Particulate</div>
        <h3>Media + HEPA filtration</h3>
        <p>5-inch media cabinets (Aprilaire, Honeywell) for standard residential. True HEPA side-streams for wildfire-season households or asthma sensitivity. Yearly filter program available.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="summit-service">
        <div class="summit-service-label">Humidity</div>
        <h3>Whole-house humidifiers</h3>
        <p>Steam and bypass humidifiers sized to home envelope. Steam for tight new builds; bypass for older homes with natural infiltration. Set-it-and-forget-it integration with your thermostat.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="summit-service">
        <div class="summit-service-label">Ventilation</div>
        <h3>ERV / HRV systems</h3>
        <p>Energy Recovery Ventilators bring in fresh outdoor air and exhaust stale air without throwing away the conditioning energy. Required in new tight-envelope builds; retrofittable in older homes.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

